\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Bounce back as yen jawboning helps. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2001\par 11:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, April 5 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were steadier in late trade on Thursday with a rebound in the yen providing some breathing space. \par \par \uc2  The yen traded at its highest level this week, hovering around 124.00 per dollar after Japanese officials made concerted efforts to talk it up. \par \par \uc2  It hit a two-and-a-half year low of 126.84 per dollar on Monday and brought down with it regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid concerns Japan's political and policy gridlock would make it difficult to snap out of its economic troubles. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said recent declines in the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were excessive and there should be some dollar correction soon, although a meaningful turnaround might not take place until the second half of this year. \par \par \uc2  "(The decline) in March had been overdone and obviously was precipitated by the dollar/yen effects," said Arthur Woo, economist at HSBC. \par \par \uc2  "But our house's view is that this will eventually reverse probably not immediately but toward the second half of the year when the relief will come for most of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region," he said. \par \par \uc2  The fact that recent hefty losses in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have raised the hackles of Asian central banks also dampened dollar demand a bit. \par \par \uc2  Korea said aggressive measures were needed to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Thailand implicitly confirmed it did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stave off baht volatility and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the peso would not drop to 52 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Markets in Korea, Taiwan and Hong Kong were closed for a one-day holiday, keeping overall activity rather subdued. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH The rupiah got little solace, hovering at just above three-week lows of 10,710 in relatively thin trading. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta dealers attributed the fall to solid corporate demand for the U.S. dollar with auto manufacturing companies, chemical and oil firms and state-owned enterprises snapping up an estimated $30 million in total. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Bank Indonesia offered cold comfort to bears as its sales of around $10-15 million in the morning from 10,700 to 10,680 failed to support the downside. They added the rupiah should breach immediate support of 10,710 in the not too distant future as companies and banks have more appetite for the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the higher SBI rates at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weekly auction reflected the authorities' effort to drain liquidity from the market and hence indirectly discourage the dollar buying. The rate on one-month SBIs rose to 15.79 percent from 15.58 percent at Wednesday's auction. \par \par \uc2  They added hopes for the IMF to approve Indonesia's reform programme and disburse a stalled $400 million loan would remove a key risk element for the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  A mission from the IMF will visit Jakarta as early as next week to review Indonesia's programme, a Japanese government official said. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht trod water cautiously, caught between strong corporate demand for the dollar and fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had dealt with the baht's recent weakness, using both spot and forward market mechanisms. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also drew mild support from news Thailand had posted a trade surplus of about $700 million in March. \par \par \uc2  "But in the immediate term, the baht's direction would depend largely on the yen. If the yen moves toward 122-123, for example, the baht's upmove could be steep because the market is excessively long of the dollar," said a Singapore dealer. \par \par \uc2  Baht funding costs offshore eased, with the one-month forward rate dipping to 35/65 satang per dollar from 60/70 satang in the morning. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR The Sing dollar tracked the higher yen and hit session highs of 1.8060, repairing most of the damage done by Monday's fall to decade lows of 1.8230. \par \par \uc2  Some cited speculation of capital inflows on talk a planned sale of Singapore's number two cellular operator Mobile One was attracting foreign operators. \par \par \uc2  Industry sources said companies expressing interest in M1 included Britain's Vodafone Group and Australia's Telestra Corp . \par \par \uc2  They estimate M1, a mobile phone arm of Keppel Telecommunications and Transportation , could be valued at between S$2-3 billion. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the rise would be short-lived as banks and companies were looking to buy dollars on dips at around 1.8050. Analysts are also less sanguine about the outlook of the Sing dollar given slowing electronics exports and the deteriorating economic situation overall. \par \par \uc2  Salomon Smith Barney cut its three-month Sing dollar forecast to S$1.83 from S$1.74. \par \par \uc2  The government is due to release a revised forecast for growth in 2001 and a preliminary first quarter growth figure on April 10. The government currently estimates the economy would grow 5-7 percent in 2001. \par \par \uc2  Salomon downgraded Singapore's growth forecast for 2001 to 5.0 percent from 5.3 percent. Analysts said lower-than-expected figures would certainly bring the dollar/Sing back to above 1.8150. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso touched a low of 50.29 before finishing onshore trading little changed from its Wednesday close of 50.16. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Manila said the peso traded in a tight range for most of the day helped by a steadier baht and a firmer yen. But the upside was capped by reluctance to unwind dollar longs on wariness about the near-term regional outlook. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was not in the market to support the peso and predicted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not fall to 52 per dollar, though it saw volatility for the next two to three months. \par \par \uc2  It \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided not to cut overnight interest rates at its weekly policy meeting despite flat inflation growth from February to March. March inflation was an expected 6.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  No trading was reported in the Taiwan dollar offshore non-deliverable forward market with financial markets in Taipei closed for the Ching Ming holiday. Trade will resume on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Singapore said one-month NDFs were steady at 0.130/150 and three-month at 0.330/375. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar on Wednesday closed at a 13-week low of 32.979 against the U.S. dollar, from Tuesday's 32.90. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Taiwan dollar's direction on Friday would depend on overnight movements in the yen and U.S. equity markets. \par \par \uc2  Any turn of events in the continuing row between China and the U.S. over a U.S. spy plane and its detained air crew would also have an impact on sentiment. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The Korean won was bid higher in thin offshore markets with Korea's financial markets closed for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  Offshore dealers expected the won to recoup some lost ground when the market reopened due to a stronger yen and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's remarks. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement on Thursday that aggressive measures were needed to stabilise the won. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at a fresh 30-month low of 1,365.20 per dollar on Wednesday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0920 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     123.75\tab  125.93\tab    +1.76\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8067\tab  1.8160\tab    +0.51\par  Taiwan dlr    32.979\tab  32.900\tab    -0.24\par  Korean won   1347.00\tab 1367.00\tab    +1.48\par  Baht\tab      45.24\tab   45.52\tab    +0.62\par  Peso\tab      50.10\tab   50.10\tab    +0.00\par  Rupiah\tab 10735.00     10595.00\tab    -1.30\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     123.75\tab  114.45\tab    -7.52\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8067\tab  1.7365\tab    -3.89\par  Taiwan dlr    32.979\tab  32.900\tab    -0.24\par  Korean won   1347.00\tab 1277.00\tab    -5.20\par  Baht\tab      45.24\tab   43.70\tab    -3.40\par  Peso\tab      50.10\tab   50.75\tab    +1.30\par  Rupiah\tab 10735.00\tab 9470.00\tab   -11.78\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4502j53\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
