\par\par \b \uc2  Philippine peso ends weaker in line with region. \b0\par\par\uc2 273 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2000\par 11:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MANILA, July 25 (Reuters) - The Philippine peso , already battered by fears of a slowing local economy and the Moslem insurgency problem, ended lower on Tuesday in step with the Thai baht and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said dollar buying by firms covering their month-end import requirements and debt settlements also pulled down the peso. \par \par \uc2  The peso settled at 44.755 to the dollar from Monday's closing of 44.60. Earlier, the peso hit a day's low of 44.76. \par \par \uc2  Turnover was higher at $118.60 million from the previous $108 million. \par \par \uc2  One trader said he believed that Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market again on Wednesday through two foreign banks. \par \par \uc2  "I think it sold $25 million at 44.65 to 44.70," the same trader said. \par \par \uc2  Other traders, however, said there was no reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the peso took a cue from the baht and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the absence of fresh local news. \par \par \uc2  The baht slid to a fresh 10-month low of 41 to the dollar in afternoon trade on Tuesday after a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official in that country said the authorities were monitoring its movement but failed to say they would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support it. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Thailand sometimes follow each other because they have the same export markets. \par \par \uc2  One trader said he expected the peso to trade between 44.65 and 44.85 to the dollar on Wednesday. ((Dolly Aglay, Manila newsroom, +632 841-8936, fax +632 817-6267; manila.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw7p0162y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
