\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA FOCUS - Chronology of Asia's financial crisis. \b0\par\par\uc2 2,366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 06:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 3 (Reuters) - The following is a chronology of Asia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  1997 \par \par \uc2  Jan 23 - Leading South Korean steel maker Hanbo Steel Corp defaults on loans, the first of a string of major corporate failures in 1997. \par \par \uc2  May 14-15 - Thailand's baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes under attack by speculators who decided Thailand's slowing economy and political instability meant it was time to sell. Thailand and Singapore jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the baht. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  is affected. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises the overnight rate to 13 percent and dumps dollars. \par \par \uc2  June 19 - Amnuay Viravan, staunchly against devaluing the baht, resigns as Thai finance minister. Resignation has immediate impact in the Philippines, where the overnight deposit rate rises to 15 percent. \par \par \uc2  June 27 - Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspends \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 16 cash-strapped finance companies and orders them to submit merger or consolidation plans. \par \par \uc2  June 30 - Thai Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh assures the nation in a televised address there will be no devaluation of the baht. \par \par \uc2  July 2 - After a failed defence of the Thai baht, Bangkok floats its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and calls on the IMF for "technical assistance". \par \par \uc2  The baht is effectively devalued by about 15-20 percent. \par \par \uc2  July 8 - Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Negara has to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to defend the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the rupiah starts to crumble. \par \par \uc2  July 14 - The IMF offers the Philippines almost $1.1 billion in financial support. \par \par \uc2  July 24 - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  meltdown. The rupiah, baht, ringgit and peso slump as confidence in the region deteriorates. \par \par \uc2  August 11 - In Tokyo, the IMF unveils a rescue package for Thailand including loans of $16 billion from the IMF and Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  August 14 - Indonesia abolishes its system of a managed exchange rate. The rupiah plunges. \par \par \uc2  August 20 - IMF approves a $3.9 billion credit for Thailand. Brunei later adds $500 million to the bail-out package, bringing it to $17.2 billion. \par \par \uc2  Sept 20 - Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad tells delegates of IMF/World Bank conference in Hong Kong that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading is immoral. \par \par \uc2  Oct 8 - Indonesia says it will ask the IMF for financial assistance. \par \par \uc2  Oct 20-23 - The Hong Kong stock market suffers its heaviest drubbing ever, shedding nearly a quarter of its value in four days on uncertainty over the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  Around this time the South Korean won also begins to slump in value. \par \par \uc2  Oct 27 - Asian jitters spill over on to world stock markets. The Dow plunges 554 points, its largest single-day point loss ever. \par \par \uc2  Oct 31 - Indonesia's IMF package is unveiled. It provides for as much as $40 billion in aid, although the front-line defence is $23 billion. \par \par \uc2  Nov 1 - Indonesia shuts down 16 troubled banks. \par \par \uc2  Nov 5 - IMF approves a $10 billion loan for Indonesia as part of the massive international package. \par \par \uc2  Nov 17 - Korea abandons its defence of the won, which rapidly falls to 1,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dec 4 - IMF approves a $21 billion loan for South Korea, part of a bail-out package that will total nearly $60 billion. \par \par \uc2  Dec 8 - Thailand closes 56 of 58 previously suspended finance companies. \par \par \uc2  1998 \par \par \uc2  Jan 8 - Representatives of the IMF and South Korea agree to a 90-day rollover of short-term debt. \par \par \uc2  Jan 12 - Peregrine, one of Asia's largest independent investment banks, collapses under a mountain of bad loans to Indonesian borrowers. Shares fall sharply in Hong Kong, China and Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Jan 15 - IMF managing director Michel Camdessus and President Suharto sign an agreement strengthening economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  Jan 22 - The rupiah collapses. It is dealt at 17,000 to the dollar at one stage. Bank Indonesia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and drives the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to end at 11,800. \par \par \uc2  Jan 27 - Indonesia announces a temporary freeze on debt servicing. \par \par \uc2  Jan 28 - International creditor banks and the South Korean government say they have agreed on a plan to exchange $24 billion of short-term debt for new loans maturing in one, two and three years. \par \par \uc2  Jan 30 - U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan warns Asia's financial crisis could spread. \par \par \uc2  Feb 14 - The IMF threatens to cut off the bail-out fund for Indonesia over its plans to introduce a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. \par \par \uc2  Feb 26 - Indonesia's borrowers and lenders begin negotiations on rescheduling at least $70 billion in private off-shore debt. \par \par \uc2  March 1 - Indonesian President Suharto says reforms prescribed by the IMF have not worked. \par \par \uc2  March 4 - The IMF approves release of the third tranche payment from Thailand's package and commends the Thai government for its economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  March 6 - IMF says its board will not discuss Indonesia's economic reform programme before April, effectively delaying a $3 billion payment initially expected by March 15. \par \par \uc2  March 23 - Indonesia nearly doubles interest rates to boost the rupiah and control inflation. \par \par \uc2  April 1 - Japan begins steps aimed at liberalising its financial markets to make them "free, fair and global" by 2001. Tokyo share prices fall, the yen dips and Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor pronounces every economic indicator bad. \par \par \uc2  April 2 - The chairman of electronics giant Sony, Norio Ohga, says the Japanese economy is on the verge of collapse. \par \par \uc2  April 3 - U.S. ratings agency Moody's lowers its outlook on Japan's sovereign debt to negative from stable. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall sharply on the news. \par \par \uc2  May 7 - Fears that riots in the Indonesian city of Medan will spill over into a wider social crisis send regional stocks into retreat. \par \par \uc2  May 11 - India stuns the world with announcement of three underground nuclear tests. Pakistan stocks tumble 3.52 percent while India's National Stock Exchange index slides 2.55 percent. \par \par \uc2  May 13 - The United States imposes economic sanctions on India and Japan suspends some loans. \par \par \uc2  May 14 - Mobs rampage through Jakarta and students demand Suharto's resignation. Indonesia's worsening crisis takes centre-stage for Asian markets, several of which suffer sharp losses. \par \par \uc2  May 15 - Suharto returns home from a Group of 15 summit in Egypt amid signs he is losing his 32-year grip on power. \par \par \uc2  May 21 - Suharto steps down under strong pressure and is succeeded by former deputy president B.J. Habibie. Markets enjoy a limited rally. Jakarta stocks surge more than three percent. \par \par \uc2  May 25 - South Korean shares plummet 6.78 percent despite a lifting of foreign ownership limits on stocks. Most other Asian markets shrug off Seoul's fall. \par \par \uc2  May 26 - Seoul's KOSPI share index continues to dive, losing a further six percent, hurt by a looming recession and labour unrest. \par \par \uc2  May 27 - Asian markets are hammered as the yen falls against the dollar and amid mounting fears of economic recession. Hong Kong stocks plunge more than five percent after Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa warns of possible recession. \par \par \uc2  May 28 - Pakistan conducts nuclear tests. \par \par \uc2  May 29 - The dollar rockets to a seven-year high of 139 yen on fears of a possible nuclear arms race in Asia and after news that Japan's unemployment rate hit 4.1 percent in April, its highest level since the end of World War Two. \par \par \uc2  Recession looms in much of Asia. Hong Kong reports that its gross domestic product contracted two percent in first quarter of 1998. About the same time Indonesia and Malaysia also report their economies contracted in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  June 1 - Pakistani stocks plunge 12.38 percent as traders get their first chance to react to sanctions imposed after the country's nuclear tests. Asian markets are hit as nuclear tension adds to a cocktail of domestic problems. \par \par \uc2  June 5 - International lenders and Indonesian companies reach a deal to reschedule the country's massive corporate debt. \par \par \uc2  June 8 - The yen falls past 140 to the dollar, sending a shudder through Asian markets. Signs that Japan and other leading economies are unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the unit sap confidence further. \par \par \uc2  June 10 - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor warns that the weak yen is having a severe impact on Beijing's foreign trade, raising fears that China may devalue its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Markets across Asia tumble, led by a near five percent fall on Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index. \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, the local dollar hits new 11-year lows and the Australian dollar plummets past 59 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  June 11 - U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin all but rules out the possibility of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by major industrial powers to bolster the yen. He says the weak yen reflects the state of the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  June 12 - Economic Planning Agency says Japan's gross domestic product fell by an annualised rate of 5.3 percent in the first quarter, signalling recession. The yen dives towards 145 to the dollar as hopes fade of official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  June 17 - The U.S. joins Japan in selling the dollar in favour of the yen. \par \par \uc2  The action takes markets by surprise and the yen rises to a high of around 134 at one stage from a level of some 142.50 prior to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  June 19 - U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin tells Reuters he is not expecting high level meetings in Tokyo to produce specific new action plans on the economy. His statements put further pressure on the yen. \par \par \uc2  June 20 - Top finance officials from the Group of Seven meet in Tokyo and vow to cooperate as appropriate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  June 23 - IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus calls the yen fall a "crisis within the crisis". \par \par \uc2  June 25 - Indonesia and the IMF sign another letter of intent - the fourth. The letter says that Indonesian GDP is expected to contract by at least 10 percent in 1998 and says the rupiah will stabilise at around 10,000 by the last quarter of 1998. \par \par \uc2  June 26 - Malaysia cuts bank statutory reserve requirements to eight percent from 10 percent. \par \par \uc2  June 29 - For the first time, South Korea closes banks, shutting five ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a major step towards meeting the mandate of the massive IMF rescue package. \par \par \uc2  July 2 - World Bank approves $1 billion loan disbursement to Indonesia, another $6 billion to arrive from international donors. \par \par \uc2  Japan Prime Minister Hashimoto and the ruling Liberal Democratic Party agree to adopt bridge bank system to tackle the country's massive bad loans. \par \par \uc2  July 4 - Foreign Minister Keizo Obuchi hints at permanent tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  July 8 - Hashimoto vows to seek permanent tax cuts and an overhaul of the tax system, but reveals no details. \par \par \uc2  July 9 - South Korea announces its intention to double this year's budget deficit in a bid to stimulate the economy. \par \par \uc2  July 12 - The ruling LDP is trounced in Upper House elections, winning only 44 seats, well short of a majority and 17 less than the 61 it had held prior to the election. \par \par \uc2  South Korea's Financial Supervisory Commission imposes involuntary restructuring on conglomerates, or chaebols, directing eight banks to pick about 16 chaebols for the exercise. \par \par \uc2  July 13 - PM Hashimoto announces his resignation and cancels planned official visits to France and the United States. \par \par \uc2  July 14 - Tens of thousands of South Korean workers down tools to protest against wage cuts and layoffs, part of the massive corporate restructuring programme. \par \par \uc2  July 15 - IMF approves immediate $1 billion loan payment to Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  July 16 - Striking Korean workers return to work, but warn of further protests if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia revises 98/99 budget to reflect the deteriorating economy, assuming a rupiah/dlr rate of 10,600. \par \par \uc2  Foreign governments announce their agreement to reschedule the country's $54.4 billion in sovereign debt. \par \par \uc2  July 20 - Protests begin at Hyundai's factory in Ulsan, striking workers pitch tents. \par \par \uc2  July 23 - Record 7.0 percent unemployment in South Korea, with more than 1.5 million unemployed. \par \par \uc2  July 24 - Obuchi wins 225 votes out of 411 in LDP voting to become LDP president and, almost certainly, prime minister. \par \par \uc2  July 29 - Moody's Investors Service warned that it may downgrade credit ratings of Thai banks due to poor results. \par \par \uc2  July 30 - Parliament formally names Obuchi prime minister; he then announces his new cabinet. \par \par \uc2  August 3 - Thailand unveils 25 point plan to salvage its economy at the start of two weeks of talks with the IMF. The plan includes measures to create jobs and stimulate the economy, and is later endorsed by the IMF. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia launches debt rescheduling agency to aid the country's debt-ridden companies. \par \par \uc2  August 6 - New Japanese prime minister Obuchi announces a plan to vary tax cuts according to existing tax brackets, and says he will ask parliament to approve cuts worth more than six trillion yen ($41.3 billion). \par \par \uc2  August 14 - The Hong Kong government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local stock market, buying stocks to squeeze out investors accused of playing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rates against the stock market. The Hang Seng Index soars 8.47 percent. \par \par \uc2  August 17 - Russia effectively devalues the rouble and declares a 90-day moratorium on some foreign debt. The next day, Asian markets slide. \par \par \uc2  Thousands of Hyundai Motor workers rally near the South Korean company's main factory complex in Ulsan, suspending production in protest over layoffs. Police stand ready for action. \par \par \uc2  August 25 - Hyundai workers return to work after a union-brokered agreement to limit number of layoffs. \par \par \uc2  August 26 - IMF approves $1 billion loan to Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  August 31 - Wall Street suffers its second-largest point loss in history, plunging 6.37 percent and sparking another series of stock market routs in Asia. \par \par \uc2  IMF approves $900 million loan payment to South Korea. \par \par \uc2  Sept 1 - Malaysia imposes new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls to contain speculation and restrict capital flows. The IMF warns that the move may undermine investor confidence. \par \par \uc2  Sept 2 - Malaysia announces that the ringgit will be fixed at 3.80 to the dollar indefinitely. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street stages its second-largest comeback in history, sparking off a rally in Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  Deputy Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim is sacked. Police state that Anwar is under investigation over allegations made in a banned book, "Fifty Reasons Why Anwar Cannot Become Prime Minister". \par \par \uc2  Sept 3 - South Korean riot police storm plants owned by Mando Machinery Corp., the country's largest auto-parts producer, to end a strike over planned mass layoffs. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du93023lf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
