\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS - Get a little help from the yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 802 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 11:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Asian markets had a mixed end to the week on Friday with a recovery in the yen offsetting lingering concerns about developments in Russia. \par \par \uc2  All the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted slight gains, ranging from 0.2 percent to just over two percent in thin volumes. \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG STOCKS LEAD THE REGION HIGHER \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks soared to a sharply higher close fuelled by short-covering sparked by a firmer Japanese yen and as fears faded of an attack on the Hong Kong dollar over the long weekend, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index rallied 564.27 points, or 8.47 percent, to end at 7,224.69. It was the ninth biggest points gain for the blue chip index. \par \par \uc2  RUPIAH STRENGTHENS AHEAD OF INDEPENDENCE DAY \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah started the day weaker but by late afternoon had posted two percent gains on its late Thursday levels as the market preapered for the long Independence Day holiday weekend in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  The early weakness in the rupiah was attributed to nervousness over the prospects for rioting or other forms of street protest to coincide with the national holiday. \par \par \uc2  On the banking front Indonesia has confirmed that the so-called interbank exchange offer by Bank Indonesia and Indonesian banks of eligible debt for new loans will go ahead as scheduled on August 25. \par \par \uc2  According to Bank Indonesia, around $2.7 billion of external bank debt, as well as a large amount of derivatives and other debt obligations, have been tendered by overseas creditors for exchange. \par \par \uc2  THE RINGGIT FIRMER BUT MARKETS WATCH STOCKS \par \par \uc2  In Malaysia the ringgit was a little firmer but traders were keeping a close eye on the KLSE after yesterday's more than five percent drop to 10-year lows. \par \par \uc2  Rating agency Fitch IBCA said it expected Malaysia's non-performing loans to peak at around 25 percent of total loans against the 20 percent peak forecast by the government's National Economic Action Council. Fitch also estimated bank recapitalisation needs at 20 billion ringgit. \par \par \uc2  Fitch IBCA assigned a long term rating of BBB-to Malaysia on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  THAILAND UNVEILS BANK REFORM PACKAGE. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was basically steady with the market digesting a bank reform package announced dueing the morning session. \par \par \uc2  The details of the package show the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effectivley going to nationalise the worst performing institutions via a debt for equity swap. \par \par \uc2  The Bangkok share market liked what it heard and rallied by over three percent with investors targetting the large capitalised banks like Bangkok Bank or Thai Farmers Bank. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's foreign reserves stood at $26.8 billion as at August 7, unchanged from a week earlier but against $27.5 billion on July 24, the Bank of Thailand said. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund, which sponsored a $17.2 billion bailout package for crisis-hit Thailand, has suggested that the country maintain minimum foreign reserves of $26-28 billion this year. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE'S ASIAN DOLLAR MARKET CONTRACTION SLOWS \par \par \uc2  In Singapore the government released figures showing the sharp deterioration in the Asian dollar market was beginning to slow. The Asian dollar market shrank by $1.5 billion in June after declining $14 billion in the first five months of 1998. \par \par \uc2  Over the 12 months of the Asian financial crisis the Asian dollar market has contracted to $484 billion from $560 billion in June last year. \par \par \uc2  PESO FIRMS WITH A LITTLE \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  HELP \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso closed firmer at its intra-day high underpinned by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar selling through foreign banks and the gains of other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There was still \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the two foreign banks. They must have sold some $50 million and they were selling at various rates," said a trader from a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  The peso ended at 43.05 to the dollar against the previous session's close of 43.21. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week has raised official interest rates sharply and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to little effect. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN MARKETS HELPED BY YEN RECOVERY. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won was a touch firmer helped by the yen's recovery while Seoul stocks rose 0.53 percentage point with traders there still worried about Russia's outlook. \par \par \uc2  Officials of South Korea's top five business groups agreed on Friday to try to sign letters of intent for restructuring by the end of August to advance the process, a business lobby group said. \par \par \uc2  The top five business conglomerates, Hyundai, Samsung, Daewoo, LG and SK Group, agreed with the government last week they would submit business swap plans by the end of August. \par \par \uc2  Government officials have said the big deal plans would involve most of the country' major industries suffering from overcapacity and overlapping investments. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e035me\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
