\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  suffer as Korean won slides. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jacqueline Wong \par \uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 1997\par 12:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 1 (Reuters) - The South Korean won fell to a new low on Monday as the market awaited details of an international bailout package for the economy. \par \par \uc2  A wave of dollar purchases pushed the won and several other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to greater depths as European markets opened. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said selling pressure stemmed from worries over the terms of the International Monetary Fund's (IMF) loan to help rescue the South Korean economy. \par \par \uc2  Some feared the IMF would want to impose very tough conditions, such as much higher interest rates which would hit economic growth. \par \par \uc2  "If conditions are very restrictive, then I would expect more pressure on the won," said Andy Tan, general manager at Standard & Poor's/MMS in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Others worried a mooted US$20 billion rescue package might not be enough to deal with South Korea's woes. \par \par \uc2  "A $20 billion dollar loan seems to have calmed the (won's) fall somewhat, but the market's fear is that this isn't enough," a dealer with a European commission house said. \par \par \uc2  The won fell to 1,182/1,188, off from 1,173 around midday. \par \par \uc2  "It was relatively quiet but some large orders (for dollars) brought about big price moves," a dealer with a local bank said, noting the general trend to reduce Asian exposure. \par \par \uc2  Tan said the next resistance point for the won against the dollar was 1,200 with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some risk of sliding past that mark, depending on the reform policies that the IMF demanded in exchange for the rescue package. \par \par \uc2  Other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also on the defensive with no reprieve due to strong corporate dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  While the two-day Asian finance ministers meeting in Kuala Lumpur, which started on Monday, was closely watched the market was disappointed it offered no immediate remedies. \par \par \uc2  The ministers stopped short of endorsing a separate standby facility to complement the IMF. \par \par \uc2  Instead, they backed an agreement reached recently in Manila that nations would provide aid on a case-by-case basis. \par \par \uc2  Traders reacted by buying the dollar against regionals. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's baht slipped to new lows with offshore falling to 41.30 against the dollar, before bouncing back to 41.05. \par \par \uc2  The onshore rate dropped to 40.80, against 40.60 earlier. \par \par \uc2  Dealers blamed the baht's offshore price fall on unabated foreign demand for dollars ahead of year-end debt requirements. \par \par \uc2  "There are so many factors for the baht's weakness. It's year-end dollar demand to repay debt and tight volume trading," said one dealer with a U.S. financial institution. \par \par \uc2  She added that Moody's Investors Service downgrade of Thai sovereign debt last week had also raised the cost of borrowing. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Indonesian rupiah fell on sustained regional weakness with spot at 3,720 against the dollar compared with 3,665/3,675 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit was hit by large dollar orders late in the session after large losses in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  Kuala Lumpur's key share index ended down 3.11 percent to 528.45 as the market reacted to trading curbs placed on five stockbroking companies over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "There was some talk that 12 other broking firms would be placed under those same curbs," one dealer said earlier. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/ringgit was lifted to a high of 3.5800 on large orders for dollars, but eased back to 3.5650. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was at 3.5250 during the early afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Philippine Peso clawed back to 34.90 against the dollar, against 35.30. Speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars at 35.30 was not confirmed. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar drifted along with regionals to 1.6045, against 1.5940/60 earlier. Dealers said the raising of prime rates by some local banks late on Monday was mostly discounted. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's dollar recouped slightly to T$32.10, up from T$32.395 but remained sluggish with its ailing stock market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks responded to the ruling Nationalist Party's surprising weekend loss in local polls, which could trigger a renewed outflow of foreign funds. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's main share index fell 5.09 percent on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was almost unchanged at 7.7309 against 7.7300 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtc101qur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
