\par\par \b \uc2  Key dates in Southeast Asia's financial crisis. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,140 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 8 (Reuter) - Indonesia's announcement on Wednesday that it will seek assistance from the International Monetary Fund was the latest development in Southeast Asia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The following are key dates: \par \par \uc2  May 14-15 - Thailand's baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hit by a massive attack by speculators who decided Thailand's slowing economy and political instability meant it was time to sell. Thailand and Singapore jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the baht. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  is affected. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises the overnight rate 1-3/4 percentage points to 13 percent and dumps dollars. \par \par \uc2  May 16 - Thailand says foreign speculators will have to "pay the price" for their attacks by buying back the baht from the more expensive swap market. \par \par \uc2  Again the Philippines is affected. The overnight rate is hiked seven percentage points to 20 percent and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells more dollars. \par \par \uc2  June 19 - Amnuay Viravan, staunchly against devaluing the baht, resigns as Thailand's finance minister. \par \par \uc2  The resignation has immediate impact in the Philippines. There, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had cut the overnight rate in the morning to 12.75 percent from 13 percent. But after the resignation announcement it jacks it back up to 15 percent. \par \par \uc2  June 27 - The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspends \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 16 cash-strapped finance companies and orders them to submit merger or consolidation plans. \par \par \uc2  June 30 - Thai Prime Minister Chavalit Yonchaiyudh assures the nation in a televised address there will be no devaluation of the baht. \par \par \uc2  July 2 - The Bank of Thailand announces a managed float of the baht and calls on the International Monetary Fund for "technical assistance". \par \par \uc2  The announcement effectively devalues the baht by about 15-20 percent. It ends at a record low of 28.80 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to defend the peso. \par \par \uc2  July 3 - The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises the overnight lending rate to 24 percent from 15 percent. \par \par \uc2  July 8 - Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bank Negara has to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to defend the ringgit. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  works and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits a high of 2.5100/10 after a low of 2.5240/50. \par \par \uc2  July 11 - The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says in a statement it will allow the peso to move in a wider range against the dollar. The IMF backs the move and Managing Director Camdessus says he would recommend the IMF board approve the Philippines' request for an extension of its Extended Fund Facility (EFF). \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the rupiah is starting to be affected. In a surprise move, Jakarta widens its rupiah trading band to 12 from eight percent. \par \par \uc2  July 14 - The IMF offers the Philippines almost $1.1 billion in financial support under fast-track regulations drawn up after the 1995 Mexican crisis. \par \par \uc2  July 24 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  meltdown. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit hits 38-month low of 2.6530 to the dollar. Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad launches bitter attack on "rogue speculators". \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar hits 32-month low of 1.4738 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar remains steady, but territory later reveals US$1 billion was spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a period of two hours on an unspecified day in July. \par \par \uc2  July 26 - Mahathir names hedge fund manager George Soros as the man responsible for the attack on the ringgit. He later brands Soros a "moron". \par \par \uc2  August 5 - Thailand unveils austerity plan and complete revamp of finance sector as part of IMF suggested policies for a rescue package. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspends 48 finance firms. \par \par \uc2  August 11 - The IMF unveils in Tokyo a rescue package for Thailand including loans totalling $16 billion from the IMF and Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  August 13 - The rupiah begins to come under severe pressure. It hits a historic low of 2,682 to the dollar before ending at 2,655. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its defence. \par \par \uc2  August 14 - Indonesia abolishes its system of managing the exchange rate through the use of a band. The rupiah plunges to 2,755. \par \par \uc2  August 16 - An unnamed Beijing source tells a local Hong Kong newspaper that China is prepared to use US$50 billion to defend the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  August 20 - IMF approves a $3.9 billion credit for Thailand. The package now totals $16.7 billion. \par \par \uc2  August 27 - Brunei adds $500 million more to Thailand's bailout package, bringing it to a total of $17.2 billion. \par \par \uc2  Sept 2 - A falling equity market helps push the Philippine peso to a record low of 30.90 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In Jakarta, the rupiah ends at 3,045. \par \par \uc2  Sept 3 - Indonesia announces measures to revive investor confidence in the country, including scrapping a 49 percent limit on foreign purchases of shares at initial public offerings and a hike in luxury taxes. \par \par \uc2  The package has little impact on the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Sept 4 - Carnage in the Philippine peso continues. It falls to a record low of 32.43 to the dollar before \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps it up slightly to end at 32.38. \par \par \uc2  Ringgit breaks through 3.0000 to the dollar barrier. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir delays several multi-billion dollar construction projects. A controversial measure to ban short selling in 100 key stocks will also be withdrawn. \par \par \uc2  Sept 16 - Indonesia says it will postpone projects worth 39 trillion rupiah in an attempt to slash the budget shortfall. \par \par \uc2  Sept 20 - Mahathir tells delegates to the IMF/World Bank annual conference in Hong Kong that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading is immoral and should be stopped. \par \par \uc2  Sept 21 - Soros says "Dr Mahathir is a menace to his own country". \par \par \uc2  Sept 22 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market gives its answer to Mahathir. The ringgit tumbles more than two percent to end at 3.1220 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Sept 30 - Ringgit fall continues. Mahathir says in Chile that 1998 Malaysian economic growth would slow due to the falling ringgit. It hits a low of 3.2150. \par \par \uc2  Oct 1 - Mahathir repeats his siren call for tighter regulation, or a total ban, on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls four percent in less than two hours to a low of 3.4080. \par \par \uc2  Oct 6 - The rupiah hits a low of 3,845. Dealers are now talking of the possibility of Indonesia having to go to the IMF for help. \par \par \uc2  Oct 8 - Indonesia says it will ask the IMF for financial assistance. \par \par \uc2  Here is the latest state of play (as of 0720 GMT) of the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in terms of their fall against the U.S. dollar since July 1. \par \par \uc2  Percentage closes have been calculated as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall against the dollar, not the dollar's rise against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab  July 1\tab Oct 8    Pct fall\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Ringgit........2.524.......3.1950.... -21.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Rupiah.........2431........3660...... -33.58\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Peso...........26.35.......35.53..... -25.83\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Baht (onshore).25.88.......36.50..... -29.10\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) Reuters Limited 1997\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dta801ej4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
