\par\par \b \uc2  S.E. Asia/Exchange Rates -3: End Of An Era For Region \b0\par\par\uc2 343 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 1997\par 08:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Economists agree that the recent developments mark something of end of an era. \par \par \uc2  'We're coming to end of a what's been a very difficult period for the region, with property and banking sectors consolidating, and hence the need for easier monetary policy,' says Angus Armstrong, chief Asia-Pacific economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  'A broader moral can be drawn about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in emerging economies,' says Derek Hargreaves, New York-based economist at J.P. Morgan. 'Fairly steadily, there is a drift away from regimes based on relatively narrow, publicized fluctuation bands,' he says in a recent report, noting the recent relaxation of the regime governing the Czech koruna. \par \par \uc2  Bernhard Eschweiler, J.P. Morgan Asia-Pacific economist in Singapore, says the Philippines risked getting itself 'caught in a trap.' \par \par \uc2  The Philippines had a free-floating exchange rate in theory. However, for the past two years the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has managed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so tightly it had barely fluctuated in the spot market. \par \par \uc2  Eschweiler said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability in the Philippines' 'good times' has exaggerated capital inflows, as investors took advantage of rich interest-rate spreads and negligible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. \par \par \uc2  But the rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  only exacerbated problems when investor sentiment deteriorates, as happened when the crisis in Thailand led foreigners to worry that its problems might be replicated in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability meant tight monetary policy, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso was circumscribed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' modest foreign reserves of around $10 billion. Higher interest rates required to defend the peso intensified pressure on a debt-laden corporate sector. \par \par \uc2  Yet the Philippines financial sector is now in some trouble because of its exposure to foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans, exactly the problem confronting Thailand now that the baht has lost more than 15% in value. \par \par \uc2  'The Philippines has a malady rather similar to Thailand's,' notes P.K. Basu, economist at Union Bank of Switzerland. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 11-07-97 \par \par \uc2  0603GMT \par \par \uc2  1135 4203 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7b051oy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
