\par\par \b \uc2  Philippines/Baht Forewarning -2: Attack On Peso \b0\par\par\uc2 194 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1997\par 09:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MANILA (AP-Dow Jones)--The Philippine monetary and fiscal authorities weren't forewarned by the Thai government that a managed float of the baht was being planned, Finance Secretary Roberto De Ocampo said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  News that a new exchange-rate system for the beleaguered baht would be implemented triggered a renewed speculative attack on the Philippine peso Wednesday, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was able to successfully fend off using open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and higher interest rates. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities weren't worried by the lack of warning, De Ocampo said. \par \par \uc2  'I'm not bothered by the fact that we were not informed about their planned announcement for the managed float of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' De Ocampo said. \par \par \uc2  The peso, he said, 'has been stable and has been able to withstand similar crises in the past.' \par \par \uc2  De Ocampo's statement appears to contradict an earlier assurance from Bank of Thailand governor Rerngchai Marakanond, who said that Thailand had warned the International Monetary Fund and Asia-Pacific central banks - including the Philippines - of its plans to alter the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 02-07-97 \par \par \uc2  0708GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7204vrn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
