\par\par \b \uc2  Manila upgrade delights analysts, market uncaring. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jonathan Thatcher \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 1997\par 12:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MANILA, May 19 (Reuter) - A ratings upgrade for the Philippines on Monday underlines what many economists and the government have been proclaiming for weeks, though largely to deaf ears. \par \par \uc2  The young cub among Asia's economic tigers has been battling for weeks, often unsuccessfully, to convince investors that the Philippines does not carry within it a virus about to erupt into the same illnesses that have weakened the Thai economy. \par \par \uc2  "I don't know why investors are still so sceptical about the Philippines economy... It's just a matter of sentiment. But it is important the Philippines shows growth is not a one-time blip," said Al Rillo, analyst at DBS Securities. \par \par \uc2  Moody's Investors Service earlier on Monday upgraded the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds and deposits and 11 of its top banks. \par \par \uc2  The news did nothing to stop the local stock market tumbling to another 17-month low, a slump triggered last month as investors decided that fears of a property glut would tear into bank profits. \par \par \uc2  Not so, argues the government, which points out that, in contrast to a number of its neighbours, major Philippines banks have a relatively low number of loans to property firms and certainly low enough to withstand a real estate crash. \par \par \uc2  "The upgrade also means that perceptive analysts could discern the strengths of an emerging market like the Philippines beyond the fashionable headlines of today that cater to fears fuelled by knee-jerk reactions and superficial comparisons," \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Gabriel Singson said. \par \par \uc2  Fears of economic chaos in Thailand have swept through the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past week, forcing Singson to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and boost interest rates to two-year highs to protect the peso. \par \par \uc2  In fact, the surge in interest rates could have an added benefit of preventing overheating from a fast-striding economy which, despite investor gloom, is still growing at 6-7 percent a year, BNP Prime East banking analyst Marco Lucena said. \par \par \uc2  "Since the start we have believed that banks have quality loans and are not on the brink of following Thailand," he added. \par \par \uc2  ING Barings analyst Joey Cuyegkeng said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was addressing concerns such as the speed of credit growth, the quality of banking assets and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lending. \par \par \uc2  "It's the overall devaluing of Asia that is keeping a lid on prices," Cuyegkeng said. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts and bankers hoped that the Moody's report would finally turn the tide in the country's favour. \par \par \uc2  "It's very good. We've been trying to show that the banking industry is not just exposed to real estate... Here's proof that we can't be compared with Thailand," said Marjo Elevado, analyst at the policy research group of the Bankers Association of the Philippines. \par \par \uc2  "This will make analysts realise that there's a night and day difference between Philippine and Thailand banks... The banking system has a very strong foundation," added Jose Facundo, president of BPI Capital Corp. \par \par \uc2  But other analysts said more proof was needed by the Philippines, which even after five years of sustained growth, had not quite managed to convince emerging market investors it was made of the right stuff. \par \par \uc2  Economic growth figures for the first quarter, expected to be published by the beginning of June, will be the next real test of the country's health. \par \par \uc2  If the figures are below expectations, the share market will certainly take another beating. \par \par \uc2  "We believe there is still more downside. If (the stock market index) breaks through 2,450 that would be scary. It could go into freefall,' Lippo Securities analyst Jidgette Valenzuela said. \par \par \uc2  The index closed on Monday at 2,509.36, a dive of 27 percent from its record high in early February. \par \par \uc2  DBS Securities' Rillo said a key factor undermining sentiment is not the economy at all but fears over who will replace President Fidel Ramos in next year's presidential election. \par \par \uc2  "In any emerging economy the political structure is important. There are still concerns that politics could undermine the economy," he added. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5j05bqo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
